# ★ NO BACKUPS INSIDE THIS TREE (operator, 2026-08-05).  Three .config.*.bak had
# been committed and published, and one still carried a product spelling that had
# been removed everywhere else -- the risk is not losing the file, it is
# publishing it, or sweeping it into a later commit without noticing.
# Backups belong in /home/user/Desktop/CatchChallenger/dev/backup/, which is not
# a git tree and therefore cannot be committed by accident.
*.bak
*.orig
*.prerename
*.prestate

# ★★ NO BACKUP LIVES IN THIS FORK (CLAUDE.md, 2026-08-05). The risk is not
# losing one -- it is PUBLISHING it: three .config.*.bak were once found tracked
# and already pushed, one still advertising a product spelling the tree had
# stopped using. Four more were found tracked on 2026-08-16 and moved to
# dev/backup/2026-08-16-fork-backups/ with their paths mirrored.
# ⚠ `*.bak` alone did NOT catch them: every one ended in a SUFFIX
# (.bak-wifi, .bak-slub, .bak-prelockup, .luna-bak-<epoch>), so the pattern that
# looked like protection matched none of the files it was written for.
# And inside files-<ver>/ this is worse than untidy: that overlay COPIES
# everything under it into the kernel build tree, so a stray backup is compiled
# and shipped rather than merely stored.
*.bak-*
*-bak
*-bak-*
.config.*bak*
config-*.bak*
